I think that's a really tough question. On one hand, I understand the idea that those who can afford it should have access to better medical care. It's only fair that people who have the means to invest in their own health and well-being should be able to do so. And let's be real, the quality of medical care can make a huge difference in people's lives. If someone has the resources to get the best treatment, they're more likely to recover from an illness or injury, and that's a good thing.

On the other hand, I also think that it's not right to leave people who can't afford it behind. Medical care should be a basic human right, not a privilege reserved for those who can pay. It's not fair to expect people to have to choose between paying their rent or paying for medical treatment. And what about people who are already struggling to make ends meet? They shouldn't have to worry about going bankrupt just because they got sick.

I think a better approach would be to find ways to make medical care more accessible to everyone, regardless of their income. That could mean increasing funding for public health programs, or implementing a single-payer system like some other countries have. It's not a simple solution, but I think it's the right one. We should be working towards a system where everyone has access to the medical care they need, not just those who can afford it." }

