completion:
Honestly, I think protectionism can be a double-edged sword. On one hand, it can help protect domestic industries and jobs by shielding them from unfair competition and giving them a chance to develop and grow. For example, if a country is heavily subsidizing its own industries, it can be hard for foreign companies to compete, which can lead to job losses and economic instability. In those cases, some level of protection can be necessary to level the playing field.

On the other hand, excessive protectionism can be harmful and even counterproductive. It can lead to higher prices for consumers, reduced innovation, and even retaliation from other countries, which can lead to a trade war. Additionally, protectionism can also stifle economic growth and development by limiting access to global markets and opportunities.

As a 26-year-old, I think it's important to strike a balance between protecting our own industries and opening up to global trade. We need to be smart about it and make sure that any protectionist measures we implement are targeted and temporary, rather than blanket policies that harm everyone. Ultimately, I think a mix of free trade and targeted protection can be the best approach." }

