completion:
Well, I've lived through a lot in my 81 years, and I've seen my fair share of conflicts and alliances. In my opinion, the old adage 'the enemy of my enemy is my friend' is a bit of a double-edged sword. On the one hand, it's true that when two parties are at odds with a common enemy, they may be willing to put aside their differences and work together to defeat that enemy. I remember during World War II, for example, the United States and the Soviet Union were allies against Nazi Germany, even though they were ideological opposites. In that sense, the phrase can be a useful tool for achieving a common goal.

On the other hand, I think it's also important to remember that just because someone is fighting against a common enemy doesn't mean they're automatically a friend. I've seen too many times in history where former enemies have made temporary alliances only to turn on each other once the common enemy is defeated. And let's not forget that the enemy of my enemy can also be a formidable foe in their own right.

So, while I think the phrase has some merit, I also think it's important to approach it with a healthy dose of skepticism and not assume that just because someone is fighting against a common enemy, they're automatically a friend. You've got to look at the motivations and values of the other party, and make sure their goals align with yours before you start calling them a friend." }

