completion:
I believe that those with the ability to pay should have access to higher standards of medical care, but with certain caveats. On one hand, it's undeniable that those who can afford it should have the opportunity to receive the best possible medical treatment, as it can significantly improve their quality of life and even save lives. Additionally, the financial burden of providing high-quality medical care to those who can afford it can actually help to drive innovation and advancements in the field, which can ultimately benefit everyone.

On the other hand, I also believe that it's essential to ensure that everyone has access to basic, essential medical care, regardless of their financial situation. This includes things like emergency services, vaccinations, and routine check-ups. It's unacceptable that people should have to worry about going bankrupt or losing their life savings because they can't afford medical treatment.

To strike a balance, I propose that we implement a tiered system, where those who can afford it can opt for premium medical services, while those who can't can still receive basic, high-quality care. This could be achieved through a combination of public and private funding, as well as innovative financing models that make medical care more accessible to everyone. Ultimately, I believe that everyone deserves access to quality medical care, but those who can afford it should have the option to upgrade to a higher level of service." }

