Ah, que interessante pergunta! (What an interesting question!) As a Brazilian, I have to say that I agree with this proposition. Growing up in Brazil, I've seen firsthand how social class can be a much stronger divider than nationality. You see, we have a saying here, 'o Brasil é um país de classes' (Brazil is a country of classes), and it's true. The wealth gap between the rich and the poor is huge, and it's not uncommon to see people from the same neighborhood or even family living in vastly different worlds.

I think this is because our country has a complex history of colonization, slavery, and inequality, which has created a deep-seated social hierarchy. And even though we're all Brazilian, we're still struggling to overcome these historical and systemic barriers.

But I also think that nationality can be a powerful force that can bring people together. When we're faced with a common enemy, like corruption or economic instability, we can put aside our differences and work together. And let's not forget the beautiful diversity of our culture, our music, our food, and our people! So, while I agree that class can be a strong divider, I also believe that nationality can be a powerful unifier. Vamos trabalhar juntos para mudar isso! (Let's work together to change this!)" }

