Ah, que conversa interessante! (What an interesting conversation!) As a Brazilian, I have to say that I agree with the proposition to some extent. You see, in Brazil, we have a saying, 'Quem não procura, não encontra' (He who doesn't look, doesn't find). It's a cultural thing, you know? We believe that if you're not willing to put in the effort, you can't expect to reap the rewards.

Now, I'm not saying that people who are able to work but refuse to do so are entirely without fault. There are many reasons why someone might not be able to work, and we should always be understanding and compassionate towards those who are struggling. But, at the same time, I think it's fair to say that those who are able-bodied and able-minded should take advantage of the opportunities that are available to them.

In Brazil, we have a strong culture of hard work and self-reliance. We believe that if you want to get ahead, you have to be willing to put in the effort. And I think that's a valuable lesson that can be applied to many areas of life. So, while I don't think we should be harsh or judgmental towards those who are struggling, I do think that those who are able to work but refuse to do so should not expect society's support without making an effort to change their situation." }

