I strongly disagree with the notion that protectionism is ever necessary in trade. As a socialist, I believe that the free market is inherently flawed and that the pursuit of profit often comes at the expense of the many. Protectionism, in my opinion, is a tool of the ruling class, used to maintain their power and control over the means of production. It's a form of economic nationalism that only serves to further entrench the existing power structures and perpetuate inequality.

Instead, I believe that we should be working towards a global system of economic cooperation and solidarity, where nations work together to address the root causes of poverty, inequality, and environmental degradation. This can be achieved through policies such as fair trade agreements, progressive taxation, and the promotion of worker-owned cooperatives.

In a truly socialist society, the concept of 'protectionism' would be obsolete, as the means of production would be collectively owned and controlled by the people, and the pursuit of profit would no longer be the driving force behind economic activity. We would be working towards a world where everyone has access to the resources they need to thrive, regardless of their nationality or geographical location." }

