I strongly disagree with the notion that the freer the market, the freer the people. In fact, I believe that the pursuit of profit and the logic of the market can often lead to the exploitation and oppression of marginalized communities and the erosion of democratic values. The idea that the market is a neutral force that can be relied upon to promote freedom and prosperity is a myth that has been perpetuated by neoliberal ideologues. In reality, the market is a powerful tool that can be used to concentrate wealth and power in the hands of the few, while leaving the many to struggle for scraps.

Furthermore, the notion that the market is the primary driver of innovation and progress is also a myth. While the market can certainly drive innovation, it is not the only force that can do so. In fact, many of the most significant advances in human history have been driven by government investment and regulation, not by the market.

In my view, true freedom and prosperity can only be achieved through a society that prioritizes the well-being of all people, not just the wealthy and powerful. This requires a strong social safety net, robust regulation of the market, and a commitment to social and economic justice. The idea that the market is the solution to all of our problems is a recipe for disaster, and we must be careful not to fall prey to its false promises." }

